    "body": "Braim Chiple. ( Image courtesy of International Copper Association. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 27, 2026\nCodelco Chief Commercial Officer Braim Chiple has resigned to join commodity trader Trafigura Group, Bloomberg reported Friday, as the Chilean state-owned copper miner reshapes its leadership under CEO Jorge Gómez.\nCarlos Olavarría, currently Codelco’s refined copper commercial manager, will succeed Chiple on Jan. 1, 2027, the miner said Thursday . Chiple has headed its commercial operations since 2024.\nThe departing executive will take a Chile-based role at Trafigura, Bloomberg reported, citing three people familiar with the matter. Trafigura declined to comment to the news agency. The trader disclosed full ownership of Chile’s Minera Altair in its half-year report.\nOlavarría will take over as Codelco seeks to diversify refined copper sales after inventories swelled in the United States ahead of possible tariffs, Bloomberg reported.\nCodelco also named James Galatoire vice-president of projects, effective Dec. 1. He has led Collahuasi’s expansion project since 2024, adding another executive from the operation Gómez previously ran.\nPaulina Andreoli, currently risk and sustainability manager at iron ore producer Compañía Minera del Pacífico, will become general manager of Codelco’s Ventanas division on Dec. 14.\nFinancial discipline\nUnder Chairman Bernardo Fontaine , Codelco is emphasizing profitable production and stronger controls as it investigates irregularities in its production records.\nThe company preliminarily identified 42,100 tonnes of copper improperly recorded as production in 2024 and 2025, with a potential accounting impact of $120 million (C$170 million) to $140 million. KPMG and EY audits remain under way, according to disclosures this month.\nCodelco had invested $32 billion over eight years while output declined, Fontaine said. He put the company’s costs at 78% above those of multinational peers, Emol reported Nov. 9, citing his interview with Chilean radio station Duna .\nThe chairman argues that private partnerships are needed to sustain production and develop unworked mineral holdings. He reiterated that approach at Asia Copper Week this month, calling for more exploration and collaboration.\nExisting partnerships include the Anillo exploration project with BHP (ASX, LSE: BHP), Nuevo Cobre with Rio Tinto (ASX, LSE: RIO), and a joint mine plan for Codelco’s Andina and Anglo American’s (LSE: AAL) neighbouring Los Bronces operation. Print\nTAG: Chile Chile mining copper copper exploration copper production\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Europe South America\nCOMPANY: Codelco Trafigura\nRepublish this article",
